WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.590
Welcome to UFI Career Studio. Answer "Tell me

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.180
about yourself". Build a concise professional story that

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.771
connects what you do now, your proof, and

00:00:10.771 --> 00:00:14.361
why this role fits. We will work through

00:00:14.361 --> 00:00:17.951
five practical steps, with examples you can adapt

00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:19.746
to your own experience.

00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:24.021
Start with your current professional direction in one

00:00:24.021 --> 00:00:27.458
or two sentences. You might say, I work

00:00:27.458 --> 00:00:30.896
in customer operations and enjoy improving the way

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:34.333
teams handle complicated requests. If you are changing

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.770
careers or beginning your first job search, name

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.208
your training or recent project instead. The goal

00:00:41.208 --> 00:00:44.645
is to give the interviewer a clear starting

00:00:44.645 --> 00:00:47.223
point, not your entire life story.

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:51.644
Choose one example that connects directly to the

00:00:51.644 --> 00:00:55.205
position. Explain what you worked on and what

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:58.765
it taught you. For example, coordinating a student

00:00:58.765 --> 00:01:02.326
event could demonstrate planning, communication, and follow through.

00:01:02.326 --> 00:01:05.886
Be clear about your own contribution without taking

00:01:05.886 --> 00:01:09.447
credit for everyone else. A specific example gives

00:01:09.447 --> 00:01:13.008
the interviewer a useful question to ask next

00:01:13.008 --> 00:01:16.123
and makes your introduction easier to remember.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.235
Connect that experience to something concrete about this

00:01:20.235 --> 00:01:23.470
opening. Refer to a responsibility, a product, or

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:26.705
a team challenge you have learned about. Say

00:01:26.705 --> 00:01:29.940
what interests you and where you could contribute.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.175
Avoid claiming that you know the company culture

00:01:33.175 --> 00:01:36.410
from a short website visit. A thoughtful reason

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.645
for applying is more convincing than saying that

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:42.475
this has always been your dream company.

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:46.592
Practice an answer of roughly sixty to ninety

00:01:46.592 --> 00:01:49.851
seconds, but treat that as a guide rather

00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:53.110
than a countdown. Record yourself once and listen

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.368
for details that do not support the story.

00:01:56.368 --> 00:01:59.627
Remove repeated points and unexplained jargon. Leave a

00:01:59.627 --> 00:02:02.886
small pause between your present work, your example,

00:02:02.886 --> 00:02:06.145
and your interest in the role. The answer

00:02:06.145 --> 00:02:09.404
should invite a conversation rather than sound like

00:02:09.404 --> 00:02:10.218
a speech.

00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:14.490
Write three prompts instead of memorizing a paragraph:

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:17.897
present, proof, and purpose. Try answering from those

00:02:17.897 --> 00:02:21.304
prompts twice using slightly different words. This helps

00:02:21.304 --> 00:02:24.711
you stay flexible if the interviewer interrupts or

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:28.118
asks a follow up. Your practice task is

00:02:28.118 --> 00:02:31.525
to record a short introduction, then check that

00:02:31.525 --> 00:02:34.932
someone unfamiliar with your background can understand what

00:02:34.932 --> 00:02:37.913
you do and why you are here.
